built.entry_point:
The look, once it is declared in the protocol:

LOOK_DECLARED=/home/user/gold-orb/lab/PROTOCOL_V3.md NUMBA_CACHE_DIR=/tmp/numba_validation_hi PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 /home/user/gold-orb/lab/r2/tracks/validation_hi/look.py --spec /home/user/gold-orb/lab/r2/tracks/validation_hi/look_spec.json --cut 2026-01-01

What it does, in order:
1. It checks the CUT before anything else. It refuses any CUT after 2026-01-01. It refuses any CUT after 2022-01-01 unless LOOK_DECLARED names a file whose text contains the word "declared". It also refuses if that look's output file already exists.
2. Only then does it append the single line to lab/results/val_access.log, in the existing format: track "autopsy_HI_look", label "look:<spec name>:<sha256[:12]>", period "val". After that it installs the read guard.
3. It runs the spec on the 2022-01-01..2025-12-31 entry windows:
   - the ten sleeves, K1, K2 and K3, and the 7 payoff variants with their references;
   - FP costs and costs x1.5;
   - "lab" (as specified) and "live" (one minute late at market, everything closed Friday 16:50 New York);
   - the portfolios P0, P1, P2, P0+orb_eth, P0+orb_btc and P0 with each payoff variant, both as the plain sum of R x stake and through the capped account (6 positions, 2 per market);
   - every comparison at equal weekly swing, with by-year weekly Sharpe;
   - the official-trades check of the ten sleeves.
4. It writes lab/results/r2/validation_hi/look/autopsy_HI_look__cut2026-01-01.json. That file holds all metrics, the spec and its sha256, the declared file and its sha256, a flag saying whether the declaration quotes the spec's sha256, and every read with its filter. Next to it goes ...__trades.parquet with every simulated trade.

The same command with --cut 2022-01-01 is an in-sample rehearsal: nothing is logged and the output goes to results/r2/validation_hi/rehearsal/.

The proofs: env -u LOOK_DECLARED NUMBA_CACHE_DIR=/tmp/numba_validation_hi PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 /home/user/gold-orb/lab/r2/tracks/validation_hi/proofs.py (about 220 s). It writes proof_a..k JSON files and an index, proofs.json.
